messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nCollege student Xiao Wang took the Postgraduate Admission Test exam in four subjects: politics, English, major one, major two. The sum of the scores of political and professional subject one is equal to the sum of the scores of the other two subjects.The sum of the scores of the political and professional subjects two is greater than the sum of the scores of the other two subjects. The scores of the professional subject one are higher than the sum of the scores of the political and English subjects.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above conditions, which of the following is the correct ranking of Xiao Wang's four subjects from high to low?\n# Options:\nA) Major subject one, major subject two, english, politics.\nB) Major subject two, major subject one, politics, english.\nC) Major subject one, major subject two, politics, english.\nD) Major subject two, major subject one, english, politics.", "<think>.\nOkay, so I need to figure out the correct ranking of Xiao Wang's four subjects based on the given conditions. Let me start by breaking down the information provided.\n\nFirst, the context mentions four subjects: politics, English, major one, and major two. Let's denote them as P, E, M1, M2 for simplicity.\n\nThe first condition says that the sum of the scores of political (P) and professional subject one (M1) is equal to the sum of the scores of the other two subjects, which are English (E) and major two (M2). So, mathematically, that would be P + M1 = E + M2.\n\nThe second condition states that the sum of the scores of political (P) and professional subject two (M2) is greater than the sum of the scores of the other two subjects, which are English (E) and major one (M1). So, P + M2 > E + M1.\n\nThe third condition mentions that the scores of the professional subject one (M1) are higher than the sum of the scores of the political (P) and English (E) subjects. So, M1 > P + E.\n\nNow, I need to figure out the order of these subjects from highest to lowest based on these conditions.\n\nLet me list out the inequalities again:.\n\n1. P + M1 = E + M2.\n2. P + M2 > E + M1.\n3. M1 > P + E.\n\nFrom condition 3, M1 is greater than the sum of P and E. That already tells me that M1 is the highest scoring subject because it's higher than both P and E individually, and their sum.\n\nNow, looking at condition 1: P + M1 = E + M2. Since M1 is already higher than P + E, let's see how that affects M2. If I rearrange condition 1, I get M2 = P + M1 - E. Since M1 > P + E, substituting that into M2 would give M2 > P + (P + E) - E, which simplifies to M2 > 2P. So M2 is greater than twice the score of P.\n\nNow, condition 2 says P + M2 > E + M1. Let's substitute M2 from condition 1 into this. From condition 1, M2 = P + M1 - E. So substituting into condition 2:.\n\nP + (P + M1 - E) > E + M1.\nSimplify the left side: 2P + M1 - E > E + M1.\nSubtract M1 from both sides: 2P - E > E.\nAdd E to both sides: 2P > 2E.\nDivide both sides by 2: P > E.\n\nSo, P is greater than E.\n\nNow, let's summarize what we have so far:.\n\n- M1 is the highest because M1 > P + E.\n- M2 is greater than 2P.\n- P is greater than E.\n\nSo, the order from highest to lowest would be M1, M2, P, E.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. From condition 1, M2 = P + M1 - E. Since M1 is very high, M2 is also quite high, but how does it compare to P?.\n\nFrom condition 2, after substitution, we found that P > E. So, P is higher than E, but how does M2 compare to P?.\n\nFrom condition 1: M2 = P + M1 - E. Since M1 > P + E, let's plug that in:.\n\nM2 = P + (something greater than P + E) - E = P + P + E + something - E = 2P + something. So M2 is definitely greater than 2P, which is more than P. So M2 > P.\n\nTherefore, the order is M1 > M2 > P > E.\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nA) Major subject one, major subject two, english, politics. → M1, M2, E, P. No, because P is higher than E.\n\nB) Major subject two, major subject one, politics, english. → M2, M1, P, E. No, because M1 is higher than M2.\n\nC) Major subject one, major subject two, politics, english. → M1, M2, P, E. Yes, this matches.\n\nD) Major subject two, major subject one, english, politics. → M2, M1, E, P. No, same as B, M1 is higher</think>.\n\nThe correct ranking is Major subject one, Major subject two, Politics, English. Therefore, my final option is: "]] 13%|#3        | 78/600 [01:30<10:26,  1.20s/it]
