messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nParents who consistently laud their children for every attempt to accomplish something, whether successful or not, actually erode the youngsters' sense of self-esteem. Children require commendation for their achievements, but if uniformly praised for both what they have accomplished and what they have merely attempted, they will eventually discount all words of commendation. In effect, such children never hear any praise at all.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which one of the following most accurately expresses the overall conclusion of the argument?\n# Options:\nA) Children will develop low self-esteem if their parents do not praise them when they succeed.\nB) Children's self-esteem will suffer if their parents uniformly praise their attempts to accomplish things regardless of their success or failure.\nC) Parents should praise their children for their achievements.\nD) Children whose actions are praised undeservedly eventually learn to discount all words of praise.", '<think>.\nOkay, so I need to figure out the answer to this logical reasoning problem. Let me read the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context says that parents who consistently praise their children for every attempt, whether successful or not, actually erode the children\'s sense of self-esteem. It mentions that children need commendation for their achievements, but if they\'re uniformly praised for both what they\'ve accomplished and what they\'ve merely attempted, they\'ll eventually stop believing the praise. So, in effect, such children never hear any praise at all.\n\nNow, the question is asking which option most accurately expresses the overall conclusion of the argument. The options are A, B, C, or D.\n\nLet me break down each option to see which one fits best.\n\nOption A: "Children will develop low self-esteem if their parents do not praise them when they succeed." Hmm, this seems related but not exactly the same. The context talks about overpraising attempts, not about not praising successes. It\'s more about the negative effect of uniform praise, not the absence of praise when they succeed. So maybe A isn\'t the best fit.\n\nOption B: "Children\'s self-esteem will suffer if their parents uniformly praise their attempts to accomplish things regardless of their success or failure." This seems to directly address the point made in the context. The context says that uniformly praising both successes and attempts erodes self-esteem because the children stop believing the praise. So B is looking correct.\n\nOption C: "Parents should praise their children for their achievements." This is more of a recommendation rather than a conclusion based on the argument. The context is pointing out the negative effect of overpraising, not suggesting how parents should act. So C might not be the conclusion but rather a counterpoint.\n\nOption D: "Children whose actions are praised undeservedly eventually learn to discount all words of praise." This also seems related. The context says that children will discount all words of commendation if they\'re praised for both attempts and achievements. So D is also a valid point, but is it the overall conclusion?.\n\nWait, the question is about the overall conclusion. The context is arguing that overpraising leads to children not believing any praise. So the conclusion is that this overpraising erodes self-esteem because the children stop valuing the praise. So the main point is that uniform praise (regardless of success) is bad for self-esteem.\n\nLooking back at the options, B directly states that self-esteem will suffer if parents uniformly praise attempts regardless of success. D talks about children discounting all praise, which is a result but maybe not the overall conclusion. The overall conclusion is about the effect on self-esteem, so B is more about the consequence, while D is about the outcome of the overpraise.\n\nWait, the context says that children will eventually discount all words of commendation, which is what D says. So D is a specific outcome, but the overall conclusion is about the effect on self-esteem. So the main conclusion is that overpraising leads to low self-esteem because the children don\'t believe the praise anymore.\n\nSo the options are:.\n\nB: self-esteem will suffer due to uniform praise regardless of success.\n\nD: children will discount all praise because they\'re overpraised.\n\nThe context\'s conclusion is that overpraising leads to children not believing any praise, which causes low self-esteem. So the overall conclusion is that self-esteem is affected because the children don\'t value the praise anymore</think>\n\nThe correct answer is B. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 16%|#6        | 98/600 [02:29<16:52,  2.02s/it]
