he third answer is the same as the second. Similarly, Candidate 3: [FC, FC, FC], written as "Forbidden City, Forbidden City".\n\nSo, to make it clear, each candidate has three answers, possibly with repeats.\n\nSo, let me list all six candidates with their three answers:.\n\n1. Candidate 1: ToH, ToH, TM.\n2. Candidate 2: TM, TM, TM.\n3. Candidate 3: FC, FC, FC.\n4. Candidate 4: ToH, TM, ?.\nWait, no, the fourth candidate is written as "the Temple of Heaven, Tiananmen Square" – but that\'s only two answers. So perhaps the third answer is missing, but that can\'t be. Wait, perhaps the way it\'s written is that each candidate\'s answers are given in order, and if they have the same answer for multiple questions, it\'s just repeated. So, for example, Candidate 4: ToH, TM, ToH? Or ToH, TM, something else?.\n\nWait, the problem says: "the fourth candidate: the Temple of Heaven, Tiananmen Square" – that\'s two answers. So perhaps the third answer is the same as the first, making it ToH, TM, ToH. Alternatively, maybe the third answer is different, but it\'s not specified. Hmm, this is confusing.\n\nWait, perhaps I should assume that each candidate has three answers, and the way they are written is that if they have the same answer for multiple questions, it\'s just listed as is. So, for example, Candidate 2: [TM, TM, TM], but written as "Tiananmen Square, Tiananmen Square" because the third answer is the same. Similarly, Candidate 3: [FC, FC, FC], written as "Forbidden City, Forbidden City".\n\nSo, perhaps the correct way to interpret each candidate\'s answers is that they have three answers, possibly with repeats, and the way they are written is just the list of their answers in order, without necessarily listing all three if they are the same.\n\nSo, for example:.\n\nCandidate 1: ToH, ToH, TM.\nCandidate 2: TM, TM, TM.\nCandidate 3: FC, FC, FC.\nCandidate 4: ToH, TM, ToH (since it\'s written as ToH, TM, but perhaps the third is ToH).\nCandidate 5: TM, FC, TM (since it\'s 