messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nIf logic comes from a priori, that is, logic transcends experience and exists before experience, then whether there is abstract logic that transcends concrete things if there is, how can we infer that many logicians often prove the transcendental nature of logic with propositions such as the sum of interior angles of triangles equal to 180 degrees-nature, especially as a symbolic system of mathematical logic.It seems that logical forms can have the self-consistency and eternal correctness of reasoning without relying on people's experience, and things in the objective world must be arranged through logical arrangement in order to make people have a clear and systematic understanding. If this is the case, we cannot help but ask how human beings, as empirical beings, know that there is no doubt that there is a logic that transcends experience, and that acknowledging the existence of transcendental logic is bound to fall into the quagmire of dogmatism or the rut of mysticism.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is the main content of this text?\n# Options:\nA) Questioning the existence of transcendental logic\nB) It shows that mysticism is undesirable\nC) Argumentation logic is a summary of experience\nD) Explaining the objective world is sometimes illogical", "<think>.\nOkay, so I need to figure out the main content of the given text based on the context provided. Let me read through the context again to understand what it's talking about.\n\nThe context starts by discussing logic coming from a priori, which means it's before experience. It mentions that logic transcends experience and exists before it. Then it asks whether there's abstract logic that goes beyond concrete things. It gives an example with the sum of interior angles of triangles being 180 degrees, which is a specific fact, but then talks about logic as a symbolic system in mathematical logic. It says that logical forms have self-consistency and eternal correctness without relying on experience. It also mentions that the objective world must be arranged logically for people to understand it systematically. .\n\nThe text then poses a question: how do empirical beings like humans know there's a transcendental logic without falling into dogmatism or mysticism. So, it's questioning how we can be sure that this transcendental logic exists without becoming dogmatic or mystical.\n\nNow, looking at the question: Which of the following is the main content of this text? The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nOption A: Questioning the existence of transcendental logic. That seems to fit because the text is asking how we know transcendental logic exists without falling into dogmatism or mysticism. So, it's questioning its existence in a way.\n\nOption B: It shows that mysticism is undesirable. The text mentions that acknowledging transcendental logic might lead to mysticism, but it doesn't explicitly say that mysticism is undesirable. It's more about the concern of falling into mysticism, not necessarily rejecting it outright.\n\nOption C: Argumentation logic is a summary of experience. The context talks about logic transcending experience, so this seems opposite. Logic isn't just a summary of experience; it's seen as existing before it.\n\nOption D: Explaining the objective world is sometimes illogical. The text doesn't say that the objective world is illogical; instead, it's about how logic can explain it systematically. So this isn't the main point</think>.\n\nThe main content of the text is questioning the existence of transcendental logic, as it discusses how humans, being empirical beings, know about its existence without falling into dogmatism or mysticism. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
