messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nBroken window effect is a psychological phenomenon, that is, if the window of a house is broken and no one repair it, and soon other windows will also be broken.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which one of the followings does not belong to broken window effect?\n# Options:\nA) If the graffiti on a wall is not cleaned, the wall will soon be full of messy and ugly things.\nB) If a tributary is polluted and not treated, it may pollute the water quality of the whole basin.\nC) Once there is rubbish in a very clean place, people will throw their rubbish away without hesitation and will not feel ashamed.\nD) If a few people in a group speak uncivilized languages and are not stopped, many people will soon speak uncivilized languages.', "<think>.\nOkay, so I have this question about the broken window effect, and I need to figure out which option doesn't belong. Let me start by understanding the concept properly.\n\nThe broken window effect is a psychological phenomenon where if something is broken and not fixed, people might start to think it's normal or not bother to fix it, leading to more broken windows or similar issues. So, it's like a domino effect where one small thing not being addressed leads to bigger problems.\n\nNow, looking at the options:.\n\nOption A: If the graffiti on a wall is not cleaned, the wall will soon be full of messy and ugly things. Hmm, this seems related. If one part of the wall is graffitied and not cleaned, others might follow, leading to more graffiti. That fits the broken window effect because it's about not addressing a small issue leading to bigger ones.\n\nOption B: If a tributary is polluted and not treated, it may pollute the water quality of the whole basin. This also makes sense. If one tributary is polluted and not fixed, it can affect the entire basin. It's a similar idea where ignoring a small pollution issue can lead to bigger environmental problems.\n\nOption C: Once there is rubbish in a very clean place, people will throw their rubbish away without hesitation and will not feel ashamed. Wait, this seems a bit different. The broken window effect is about not fixing something, leading others to think it's okay. But here, it's about people not feeling ashamed to throw rubbish in a clean place. It's more about social norms or behavior rather than the physical state of the environment. So maybe this doesn't fit the broken window effect.\n\nOption D: If a few people in a group speak uncivilized languages and are not stopped, many people will soon speak uncivilized languages. This does fit. If a few people start behaving uncivilized and aren't corrected, others might follow suit, leading to more uncivilized behavior. That's exactly the broken window effect.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the broken window effect is about not addressing small issues leading to bigger ones. Options A, B, and D all describe situations where ignoring a small problem can cause a larger one</think>\n\nThe correct answer is C. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
