messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nIn recent years, community banks in some big cities have been closed frequently. At the same time, a series of new banks, such as unmanned banks, 5G banks and smart banks, keep emerging. Bank branches are bidding farewell to cold glass counters and metal benches. The traditional transaction processing functions of bank branches are weakened, and customized services, product experience, social interaction and other functions are increasingly prominent. As a result, some experts predict that traditional bank branches will disappear within 20 years.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the followings, if true, best supports the expert's view?\n# Options:\nA) Customers need to enter the door to take the number, wait for the number, to deal with a simple business cost a long time\nB) The introduction of artificial intelligence and other technological means has changed people's impression of bank branches\nC) Complex business must be signed face to face at the bank branch, such as account opening, account cancellation, etc., must be handled by myself and manual audit\nD) Online banking, mobile banking and other emerging, bank outlets as the main channel of service is weakening", '<think>.\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option best supports the expert\'s view that traditional bank branches will disappear within 20 years. Let me start by reading the context again to make sure I understand it.\n\nThe context says that community banks in big cities are closing a lot. At the same time, new types of banks like unmanned, 5G, and smart banks are popping up. Traditional bank branches are moving away from glass counters and metal benches, which means the transaction processing functions that were done in person are being reduced. Instead, they\'re emphasizing things like customized services, product experiences, and social interactions. Experts think these traditional branches might disappear in 20 years.\n\nNow, the question is asking which option, if true, best supports this expert view. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: "Customers need to enter the door to take the number, wait for the number, to deal with a simple business." Hmm, this seems like it\'s talking about the process of getting a number at a branch, which might be about waiting times. But the context mentions that traditional transaction functions are being weakened, so maybe this is an example of something that\'s becoming less necessary because of technological changes. But I\'m not sure if this directly supports the expert\'s view about the disappearance of branches.\n\nOption B: "The introduction of artificial intelligence and other technological means has changed people\'s impression of bank branches." This seems relevant because the context mentions new technologies like AI, 5G, and smart banks. If people\'s perceptions are changing, they might not need to visit physical branches as much, which could lead to the decline of traditional branches. This seems like it supports the expert\'s view.\n\nOption C: "Complex business must be signed face to face at the bank branch, such as account opening, account cancellation, etc., must be handled by myself and manual audit." This is about the need for in-person handling of complex tasks. If these can\'t be done online, that might mean people still need to go to branches. But the context says that some functions are moving away from branches, so maybe this is a point against the expert\'s view. Or perhaps it\'s a reason why branches might still exist. I\'m a bit confused here.\n\nOption D: "Online banking, mobile banking and other emerging, bank outlets as the main channel of service is weakening." This seems to suggest that online and mobile banking are becoming the main channels, which would mean traditional branches are being used less. That directly supports the expert\'s prediction that branches will disappear.\n\nWait, but let me read the options again carefully. Option D says "Online banking, mobile banking and other emerging, bank outlets as the main channel of service is weakening." Hmm, the wording is a bit unclear. It might mean that online and mobile are becoming the main channels, which would weaken the role of traditional branches. So that supports the expert\'s view.\n\nComparing B and D: B is about changing perceptions due to technology, which could lead to less need for branches. D is about the main service channels shifting, which directly implies that branches are being used less.\n\nSo both B and D seem relevant. But the question is which one best supports the expert\'s view. The expert\'s view is that traditional branches will disappear because they\'re moving away from transaction functions and emphasizing other services. So D is more directly about the shift in service channels, which is a key point. B is more about how technology changes perceptions, which might be a contributing factor but maybe not the direct cause.\n\nWait, but the context also mentions that new banks like unmanned, 5G, and smart banks are emerging. So D is talking about online and mobile banking as the main channels, which are part of the technological changes. So maybe both B and D are correct, but the question is which one is the best.\n\nLooking back at the options, D says that online banking, mobile banking, and other emerging bank outlets are the main service channels, which are weakening. That means traditional branches are no longer the main way banks provide services, which supports the expert\'s view that they\'ll disappear.\n\nOption B is about changing perceptions, which is a result of technology but not directly about the shift in service channels</think>.\n\nThe best answer is D. .\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**.\n\n1. **Understanding the Expert\'s View:** The expert predicts that traditional bank branches will disappear within 20 years due to a shift away from transaction functions and an emphasis on other services.\n\n2. **Analyzing the Options:**.\n   - **Option A:** Discusses waiting times, which relates to transaction efficiency but doesn\'t directly support the branch disappearance.\n   - **Option B:** Talks about technological changes altering perceptions, which indirectly supports the shift away from branches.\n   - **Option C:** Highlights the need for in-person complex tasks, suggesting branches might still be necessary.\n   - **Option D:** States that online, mobile, and emerging bank outlets are becoming the main service channels, directly indicating a decline in traditional branches.\n\n3. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 56%|#####6    | 336/600 [08:12<05:28,  1.25s/it]
