      "text": " A\n</think>\n\n**Answer: A**\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe proposal suggests adjusting the calendar so that January 1 falls on a Sunday every year. This adjustment would involve adding an extra day every fourth year, similar to how leap years work. However, the current system already accounts for this with leap years, which every four years adds a day to February. The key issue is that the current calendar does not align the days of the week consistently across years, leading to shifting days for anniversaries and holidays.\n\n- **Option A:** Employed people whose strict religious observances require them to refrain from working every seventh day. This group would continue to face scheduling conflicts because the calendar adjustment doesn't change the day of the week for their days off. If January 1 is a Sunday, their next day off would still be a Monday, not aligning with their religious observance.\n\n- **Option B:** People with birthdays or anniversaries on December 30 or 31. The adjustment affects January 1 but not December 31, so their anniversaries would still shift similarly to how they do now.\n\n- **Option C:** People who need to plan events several years before they occur. The calendar adjustment would make long-term planning more consistent, reducing conflicts.\n\n- **Option D:** Employed people with three-day breaks when holidays fall on Mondays or Fridays. The adjustment would align holidays more consistently, reducing the need for such breaks.\n\nThus, the group most likely to face continued scheduling conflicts is **A**.",
