l wrong must not be closed." So, the door to all wrong must not be closed. That\'s equivalent to not closing all wrong doors, which is the contrapositive. So, that\'s correct.\n\nOption D: To accept the truth, we must reject wrong. Hmm, that\'s a bit more abstract. The original statement is about closing doors, so maybe D is a bit of a stretch. It\'s more about the relationship between truth and wrong, but the original is more about the action of closing doors leading to truth being shut out.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The original statement is: If you close all wrong doors, truth is shut out. So, closing wrong doors leads to truth being shut out. So, if you want truth to be not shut out, you shouldn\'t close all wrong doors. So, the contrapositive is: If truth is not shut out, then you don\'t close all wrong doors.\n\nSo, looking at the options:.\n\nA: If truth is shut out, then you close all wrong doors. That\'s the converse, which isn\'t necessarily true based on the original statement.\n\nB: Truth can not be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed. Wait, that\'s a bit confusing. Let me parse it again. It\'s saying that unless all wrong doors are closed, the truth can\'t be shut out. So, if you don\'t close all wrong doors, the truth can\'t be shut out. That\'s the contrapositive, so B is correct.\n\nC: If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed. That\'s the contrapositive as well, so C is correct.\n\nWait, but both B and C seem to be correct. But let me check the wording again.\n\nOption B: "Truth can not be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed." So, unless all wrong doors are closed, the truth can\'t be shut out. That\'s the contrapositive.\n\nOption C: "If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed." So, if the truth is not shut out, then you don\'t close all wrong doors. That\'s the contrapositive as well.\n\nWait, but in logical terms, both B and C are expressing the 