messages:   98%|#########7| 586/600 [15:03<00:22,  1.58s/it][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nWhen you are about to graduate, a certain class will select outstanding graduates, and discussions are ongoing within the class. Class leader: Either Li Xue is rated as an outstanding graduate or Wang Lei is rated as an outstanding graduate. Youth League Party Secretary: I disagree.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following accurately expresses the opinion of the party secretary?\n# Options:\nA) Both li xue and wang lei were rated as outstanding graduates\nB) Neither li xue nor wang lei can be rated as outstanding graduates\nC) Either li xue and wang lei are both rated as outstanding graduates, or neither li xue nor wang lei can be rated as outstanding graduates\nD) Li xue was rated as an outstanding graduate, wang lei cannot be rated as an outstanding graduate', '<think>.\nOkay, so I have this logic problem to solve. Let me read it carefully.\n\nThe context says that when a class is about to graduate, they\'re discussing whether to select outstanding graduates. The class leader says that either Li Xue is rated as outstanding or Wang Lei is. Then the Youth League Party Secretary disagrees with this.\n\nThe question is asking which option accurately expresses the party secretary\'s opinion. The options are A, B, C, or D.\n\nFirst, I need to break down the statements. The class leader\'s statement is "Either Li Xue is rated as an outstanding graduate or Wang Lei is rated as an outstanding graduate." In logical terms, that\'s like saying (Li ∨ Wang). The party secretary disagrees with this, so the negation of the class leader\'s statement would be the party secretary\'s opinion.\n\nIn logic, the negation of (A ∨ B) is (¬A ∧ ¬B). So, the party secretary is saying that it\'s not the case that Li is outstanding, and it\'s also not the case that Wang is outstanding. So, both Li and Wang cannot be outstanding.\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nA) Both were rated. That\'s (Li ∧ Wang). But the party secretary disagrees with the class leader\'s (Li ∨ Wang), so A can\'t be right because the party secretary\'s opinion is the negation, not the same as the class leader\'s.\n\nB) Neither can be rated. That\'s (¬Li ∧ ¬Wang). That matches the negation of the class leader\'s statement, so B seems correct.\n\nC) Either both are rated or neither is. That\'s (Li ∧ Wang) ∨ (¬Li ∧ ¬Wang). This is actually the same as the class leader\'s statement or its negation. But the party secretary only disagrees with the class leader\'s (Li ∨ Wang), so the party secretary\'s opinion is specifically (¬Li ∧ ¬Wang), not the broader option C.\n\nD) Li is rated, Wang isn\'t. That\'s (Li ∧ ¬Wang). But the party secretary\'s opinion is that neither can be, so D isn\'t correct</think>.\n\nThe party secretary\'s opinion is the negation of the class leader\'s statement, meaning neither Li Xue nor Wang Lei can be rated as outstanding graduates. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
